 "title": "The morning pond",
 "brief": "A pond at first light, nearly still. The water is the page itself, cooled toward one of the theme's blue-green hues, and it breathes in long, low swells: the morning Thoreau calls the awakening hour. A low sun, the warmest chart hue, rises a little and sinks again behind a veil of mist that drifts up from the surface in faint ink. In the foreground a single reed stands in the water, the one upright thing in the scene, and around it five rings spread and fade in turn, as if something has just touched the surface.\n\nEverything else is left out. That is the point of the chapter: simplify, simplify. Few elements, wide empty water, and slow motion that never repeats exactly the same way twice in a minute. The camera sways a little, like someone standing on the shore deciding to stay.",
